DIRB                                                           [3.0]
----

Format:      DIRB [/S] [/H] [/W] [/P] [/2] [compound-filespec]

Purpose:     Displays the names of files on disk,  with the sizes in
        bytes.

Use:         DIRB is identical to the DIR command (see HELP DIR)  in
        every respect except one:  the file sizes,  the total size of
        the  listed  files and the free  space  figure  are  always
        printed in bytes.  DIR prints the figures of 10K and over  in
        kilobytes (a threshold that can be changed with the DIRK envi-
        ronment item,  which can also limit the kilobytes to the  to-
        tals line); DIRB ignores that item.

Examples:

        DIRB

             All  filenames  and  directory  names  in  the  current
        directory of the current drive will be printed,  with  their
        sizes in bytes. This might be as follows:

              Volume in drive A: is MSX-DOS 2
              Directory of A:\

             NEXTOR   SYS r    4455 86-06-19  2:45p
             COMMAND3 COM r   10496 86-06-19  2:46p
             UTILS           <dir>  86-06-19  2:50p
             HELP            <dir>  86-06-19  2:50p
              14951 bytes in 2 files   227328 bytes free

        DIRB B:\HELP/W

             A  'wide'  directory format has been requested  of  the
        HELP directory on drive B:.  The sizes are only shown in the
        totals line.
